<a id="sec-3"></a>

### Chapter 3 / Exercise 2: Is depth 8 better than depth 2?

The same five seeds were used for both settings, so the comparison is paired by seed.

<a id="tbl-3"></a>

**Table 3.** Paired comparison, depth 8 minus depth 2

| metric | mean_a | mean_b | mean_diff | ci_low | ci_high | t | df | p_value | cohen_d | n_pairs |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| test_accuracy | 0.8090 | 0.7000 | 0.1090 | 0.0929 | 0.1251 | 18.8323 | 4.00 | 0.0000 | 8.4221 | 5 |
| f1 | 0.7690 | 0.6600 | 0.1090 | 0.0929 | 0.1251 | 18.8323 | 4.00 | 0.0000 | 8.4221 | 5 |

*a = depth 8, b = depth 2; paired t-test over shared seeds, 95% interval of the mean difference a - b*

```
Paired t-test: t = 18.8323, df = 4.00, p = 4.682e-5, mean diff = 0.1090, 95.0% CI [0.0929, 0.1251], Cohen d = 8.4221, Hedges g = 6.7198
```

> **Note.** Five seeds give a wide interval; treat the p-value as a rough guide, not a verdict.

